---

<BlogLayout
  title="House Hacking With an FHA Loan on Multi-Family Properties | Cliffco Mortgage"
  description="House hacking with an FHA loan on a multi-family property lets first-time investors buy with a low down payment and have tenants help cover the mortgage. Here's exactly how it works."
  type="article"
>
  <Breadcrumbs trail={[
    { name: "Home", href: "/" },
    { name: "Blog", href: "/mortgage-guides/blog/" },
    { name: "House Hacking With an FHA Loan", href: "/mortgage-guides/blog/house-hacking-fha-loan-multi-family-property/" },
  ]} />

  <article class="container-prose pt-12 pb-16">

    <h1 style="font-size: var(--text-display-lg);">House Hacking With an FHA Loan: How to Buy a Multi-Family Property and Let Tenants Help Pay the Mortgage</h1>

    <ArticleImage />

    <p class="mt-8 font-bold">If This Is You, Keep Reading</p>

    <p class="mt-4">If you want to get into real estate investing but don't have a large down payment sitting in a savings account, and you're open to living in one unit of a two to four unit building for a year or two, house hacking with an FHA loan on a multi-family property is the most accessible entry point in residential real estate financing.</p>

    <h2 class="mt-10">What Most People Get Wrong About FHA Loans</h2>

    <p class="mt-4">Most people think FHA loans are strictly for first-time homebuyers purchasing a single-family home. That's not accurate. FHA will finance two, three, and four unit properties, and as long as you occupy one unit as your primary residence, the entire building qualifies under owner-occupied terms. That means a significantly lower down payment and a rate much closer to primary residence pricing. The tenants in the other units help cover your housing cost, and in many cases eliminate it almost entirely.</p>

    <h2 class="mt-10">The Owner-Occupancy Clock and Why Lenders Watch It</h2>

    <p class="mt-4">Here's where investors get into trouble. FHA requires you to move in within 60 days of closing and occupy the property as your primary residence for at least 12 months. That's not a suggestion. If you close on a multi-unit property with FHA financing and immediately treat it as a pure investment, you've violated the occupancy requirement. That's a serious compliance issue, not a technicality.</p>

    <p class="mt-4">The conflict is this: your instinct as an investor is to optimize the asset from day one. FHA is lending you money under the assumption you're a homeowner first and an investor second. Most lenders won't tell you that until it becomes a problem.</p>

    <p class="mt-4">After 12 months, you're free to move out, convert your unit to a rental, and repeat the process with another FHA purchase on a new primary residence, as long as you can justify the move as a legitimate change in circumstances.</p>

    <h2 class="mt-10">How the FHA Multi-Family Numbers Actually Work</h2>

    <p class="mt-4">FHA loans go up to four units. The math is straightforward once you understand the structure.</p>

    <p class="mt-4">You purchase a multi-unit property using a low FHA down payment. Your total monthly payment includes principal, interest, FHA mortgage insurance, taxes, and insurance. The rental income from the units you don't occupy offsets a significant portion of that payment. FHA allows lenders to count a percentage of projected rental income toward your qualification, which helps your debt-to-income ratio and reduces the effective housing cost you're carrying.</p>

    <p class="mt-4">In markets where rents are strong relative to purchase prices, the numbers can work in your favor quickly. The goal is to get into a property where the combined rent from other units brings your out-of-pocket housing cost down to a level that's competitive with, or lower than, what you'd pay to rent a comparable space elsewhere.</p>

    <p class="mt-4">FHA loan limits vary by county and unit count. Check your county's specific limit before you build your search around a purchase price, because the ceiling differs meaningfully between standard and high-cost areas.</p>

    <h2 class="mt-10">What to Do, In Order</h2>

    <ol class="mt-4 list-decimal list-inside space-y-2">
      <li><strong>Confirm you meet FHA's basic requirements first.</strong> Minimum credit score thresholds apply, and your debt-to-income ratio will be evaluated carefully. Know where you stand before you start shopping.</li>
      <li><strong>Get pre-approved before you look at properties.</strong> Multi-family properties in desirable markets move quickly. You need to know your exact purchase ceiling before you start touring buildings.</li>
      <li><strong>Run the rent numbers for every unit before you make an offer.</strong> Pull comparable rents in the area, not the seller's projections. Underwrite conservatively, not at 100% occupancy.</li>
      <li><strong>Budget for the owner-occupancy year.</strong> Plan to actually live there. Don't buy a property in a neighborhood you won't spend 12 months in. The strategy only works if you execute it honestly.</li>
      <li><strong>Talk to a CPA before you close.</strong> Understanding how to split personal versus rental use for tax purposes matters from month one. The depreciation schedule starts the day you close.</li>
      <li><strong>Plan your next move before you move out.</strong> Line up your next purchase opportunity several months before the occupancy clock clears so you're ready to act when the time comes.</li>
    </ol>

    <h2 class="mt-10">Run the Numbers Before You Pick the Building</h2>

    <p class="mt-4">Before you make an offer on any multi-family property, use Cliffco's Payment Estimator to see exactly what your total monthly payment looks like at different purchase prices and down payment amounts. Then run the full cash flow picture through the Budget Builder with realistic vacancy and maintenance assumptions. If the deal works at conservative occupancy with one unit being yours, you've found something worth pursuing.</p>

    <p class="mt-4">The down payment is low. The barrier is knowing whether the numbers work at conservative occupancy before you fall in love with the building.</p>

    <!-- Embedded lite Payment Estimator -->
    <div class="mt-12 border border-[var(--color-accent)]/25 rounded-2xl overflow-hidden">
      <div class="bg-[var(--color-accent)]/8 px-6 py-5 border-b border-[var(--color-accent)]/15">
        <p class="eyebrow text-xs mb-1">Quick check</p>
        <h2 class="text-xl font-semibold text-[var(--color-ink)]">Estimate your monthly payment.</h2>
        <p class="text-sm text-[var(--color-text-muted)] mt-1">Enter the numbers for any property to see your estimated monthly payment before you make an offer.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="p-6">
        <div class="grid gap-4 sm:grid-cols-2 lg:grid-cols-3">
          <div>
            <label class="block text-sm font-medium text-[var(--color-ink)] mb-1">Home price ($)</label>
            <input id="art-pe-price" type="number" value="500000" min="50000" step="5000" class="art-calc-input" />
          </div>
          <div>
            <label class="block text-sm font-medium text-[var(--color-ink)] mb-1">Down payment (%)</label>
            <input id="art-pe-down" type="number" value="20" min="3" max="50" step="1" class="art-calc-input" />
          </div>
          <div>
            <label class="block text-sm font-medium text-[var(--color-ink)] mb-1">Interest rate (%)</label>
            <input id="art-pe-rate" type="number" value="7.0" min="1" max="15" step="0.125" class="art-calc-input" />
          </div>
          <div>
            <label class="block text-sm font-medium text-[var(--color-ink)] mb-1">Loan term</label>
            <select id="art-pe-term" class="art-calc-input">
              <option value="30">30-year fixed</option>
              <option value="20">20-year fixed</option>
              <option value="15">15-year fixed</option>
            </select>
          </div>
          <div>
            <label class="block text-sm font-medium text-[var(--color-ink)] mb-1">Annual taxes ($)</label>
            <input id="art-pe-taxes" type="number" value="6000" min="0" step="100" class="art-calc-input" />
          </div>
          <div>
            <label class="block text-sm font-medium text-[var(--color-ink)] mb-1">Annual insurance ($)</label>
            <input id="art-pe-ins" type="number" value="2400" min="0" step="100" class="art-calc-input" />
          </div>
        </div>
        <button id="art-pe-btn" class="btn btn-primary mt-5">Estimate Payment</button>

        <div id="art-pe-result" class="hidden mt-6">
          <div class="p-5 bg-[var(--color-mist-gray)] rounded-xl">
            <div class="grid gap-4 sm:grid-cols-3">
              <div>
                <p class="text-xs text-[var(--color-text-muted)] uppercase tracking-wide">Principal &amp; Interest</p>
                <p id="art-pe-pi" class="text-3xl font-bold text-[var(--color-accent)] mt-1"></p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-xs text-[var(--color-text-muted)] uppercase tracking-wide">Taxes &amp; Insurance</p>
                <p id="art-pe-ti" class="text-2xl font-bold text-[var(--color-ink)] mt-1"></p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-xs text-[var(--color-text-muted)] uppercase tracking-wide">Total Monthly Payment</p>
                <p id="art-pe-total" class="text-2xl font-bold text-[var(--color-ink)] mt-1"></p>
              </div>
            </div>
            <p id="art-pe-note" class="mt-3 text-xs text-[var(--color-text-muted)]"></p>
          </div>

          <div class="mt-4 p-5 bg-[var(--color-rich-black)] rounded-xl flex flex-col sm:flex-row sm:items-center gap-4 justify-between">
            <div>
              <p class="text-white font-semibold">Know your number before you make an offer.</p>
              <p class="text-white/70 text-sm mt-1">A Cliffco loan officer can lock a rate and get you pre-approved the same day.</p>
            </div>
            <a href="/get-started/" class="btn btn-primary !bg-white !text-[var(--color-rich-black)] shrink-0 whitespace-nowrap">Talk to a loan officer</a>
          </div>

          <p class="mt-4 text-sm text-center text-[var(--color-text-muted)]">
            Want to model other scenarios? <a href="/mortgage-guides/calculators/payment-estimator/" class="text-[var(--color-accent)] font-medium">View the full payment estimator →</a>
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

  </article>
</BlogLayout>

<style>
  .art-calc-input {
    width: 100%;
    border: 1px solid var(--color-soft-gray, #d1d5db);
    border-radius: 0.5rem;
    padding: 0.5rem 0.75rem;
    font-size: 0.875rem;
    color: var(--color-ink);
    background: white;
    outline: none;
    transition: border-color 0.15s;
  }
  .art-calc-input:focus {
    border-color: var(--color-accent);
    box-shadow: 0 0 0 3px color-mix(in srgb, var(--color-accent) 15%, transparent);
  }
</style>

<script>
  const peBtn = document.getElementById("art-pe-btn")!;
  peBtn.addEventListener("click", () => {
    const price = +(document.getElementById("art-pe-price") as HTMLInputElement).value;
    const downPct = +(document.getElementById("art-pe-down") as HTMLInputElement).value;
    const rate = +(document.getElementById("art-pe-rate") as HTMLInputElement).value;
    const term = +(document.getElementById("art-pe-term") as HTMLSelectElement).value;
    const taxes = +(document.getElementById("art-pe-taxes") as HTMLInputElement).value;
    const ins = +(document.getElementById("art-pe-ins") as HTMLInputElement).value;

    const loanAmount = price * (1 - downPct / 100);
    const r = rate / 100 / 12;
    const n = term * 12;
    const pi = r === 0 ? loanAmount / n : loanAmount * (r * Math.pow(1 + r, n)) / (Math.pow(1 + r, n) - 1);
    const ti = (taxes + ins) / 12;
    const total = pi + ti;
    const fmt = (v: number) => "$" + Math.round(v).toLocaleString("en-US");

    (document.getElementById("art-pe-pi") as HTMLElement).textContent = fmt(pi);
    (document.getElementById("art-pe-ti") as HTMLElement).textContent = fmt(ti);
    (document.getElementById("art-pe-total") as HTMLElement).textContent = fmt(total);
    (document.getElementById("art-pe-note") as HTMLElement).textContent =
      `Loan amount: ${fmt(loanAmount)} | Down payment: ${fmt(price * downPct / 100)}. Estimate only - does not include PMI, HOA, or other costs.`;
    (document.getElementById("art-pe-result") as HTMLElement).classList.remove("hidden");
  });
</script>
